 "observations": [
  "The page number «143» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 32», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03 in smaller bold type, as on pp. 140-142 (JP2 0172-0174). The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the last title in two lines, «C 58v, O 116r,» and «N 28r.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 141 (JP2 0173), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Catur eladed (C)», the left parenthesis of «(C)» printed in two pieces; the subheads «Augmentum proprietatum omnium1)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium1)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio ascensus Saturni examinata2)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Saturni»; «Latitudo Saturni recedentis», without a note, over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L08), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L09, the units, in bold.",
  "L10-L39. Checked as on p. 138 (JP2 0170): the Sublimatio is 8s 4° 55′ less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, to 8s 4° 55′, the apogee itself, at 180°. The Examinatio argumenti agrees with the equation of the epicycle of radius 6 of p. 141 (JP2 0173) to within 1.1′ in every row, and is 0 at 180°. The Latitudo secunda is 5° · sin x to within 0.7″ in every row except at 155° and 158°, each 1.1″ from the computation: 2° 6′ 46″ for 2° 6′ 47.1″, where p. 138 has 2° 6′ 47″ at 25°, and 1° 52′ 24″ for 1° 52′ 22.9″ (note h), where p. 138 has 1° 52′ 23″ at 22°; the 23 of O and N in note h agrees with both.",
  "L10-L39, the other columns: the Examinatio centri for 180° − y is that of y on pp. 138-139 (JP2 0170-0171) except at 151°, 163°, 164°, 166° and 179°: 4° 10′, 2° 30′, 2° 22′, 2° 3′ and 0° 9′ against 4° 9′, 2° 29′, 2° 21′, 2° 4′ and 0° 8′ at 29°, 17°, 16°, 14° and 1°. At 179° the edition prints C's 9 (note g); O's 8 is the value of 1°. The Statio prima increases from 3s 25° 20′ to 3s 25° 30′, its greatest value, reached at 178°, by 1′ every second or third degree. The Latitudo prima is 1° 12′ to 152°, 1° 11′ to 172° and 1° 10′ from 173° on, its least value.",
  "L18, the second argument, «2 | 21» for 6s 21°: see the critical note. The arguments of the two columns add up to 12 signs in every other row.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: the editor's readings agree with the rows around them where the variants depart: the Sublimatio 8s 3° 35′ at 155° (note b), required by the Examinatio argumenti 2° 40′ of the row; the Examinatio argumenti 3° 3′ at 151° (note c), C's 2° 3′ being a degree short; the Examinatio centri 3° 3′, 2° 38′ and 1° 46′ at 159°, 162° and 168° (notes d, e, f), the values at 21°, 18° and 12°. Note a: the last argument, 6s 0°, is N's; C and O have 5.",
  "Italics: «om.». N01: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed; the left parenthesis of «(Diminutio)» printed in two pieces, as in L05; the final o of «Sublimatio» stands a little below the line. N03, «xxxo N», as on p. 139 (JP2 0171, N07)."
 ],
